./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:6:Choose storage for three kinds of data: application records, searchable knowledge, and original files. Moving one does not move the others. Storage placement also does not determine where a model provider or connector processes a request; include those destinations in your residency assessment.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:13:| Knowledge database: extracted text, embeddings, search indexes, and crawled content | `KNOWLEDGE_DATABASE_URL` | **Settings > Data residency > Knowledge database** |
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:22:Provision the database and credentials before changing Tale. The application database needs a role with permission to apply its schema migrations. The knowledge database needs `vector` installed; `pg_search` enables the BM25 part of hybrid search. A target with only pgvector provides vector search without that keyword leg. Tale prepares knowledge schemas and tables but does not install extensions on a database you own.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:41:4. Index a controlled document, search for a known phrase, and verify that pre-existing content required by your users remains available.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:47:In **Embedding model**, choose the provider and stored credential, then the model. **Model** lists the embedding models the provider's catalog carries and fills the vector width the catalog states. A provider that offers no embedding model at all, such as Anthropic, cannot be chosen: the row reads **Cannot embed**, and you pick another provider. Where Tale knows no vector width for the provider, type the model tag (for Azure OpenAI, your deployment name) and the vector width that model produces. That applies to a shipped provider whose catalog lists no embedding model, to a provider your organization defined itself whose model listing carries none, and to Azure OpenAI, whose deployments carry your own names. The provider's [`embedding` declaration](/self-hosted/configuration/providers#what-a-connector-declares) decides between the two, and saving refuses a provider declared unable to embed however the request arrives. If Tale cannot check the declarations, the section says so and offers **Try again**; until the check answers, no model can be chosen. An optional base URL selects an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Without a configured embedding model, knowledge indexing and search cannot operate normally.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:51:`embedding.json` can set `minSimilarity`, the assistant search's vector-leg floor; its default is `0.45`. The settings form preserves this file value but does not expose a field for it. Tune it against representative queries. REST knowledge search applies a floor only when its request supplies one; this is not a universal score threshold for all searches.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:57:- `maxConcurrentRequests` (1 to 64, default 3) is how many embedding requests to this model Tale keeps in flight at once for the organization. Document indexing, the indexing of incoming email, website scans and searches share this limit. Further requests wait in arrival order, except that a search query goes ahead of waiting indexing batches. Each Tale process counts separately, so the API and every worker replica can each reach the limit. A lower value takes effect at once; a higher one once the requests started under the old value have finished. On a server that computes one request at a time, a higher value adds no load; each request only waits longer.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:59:- `maxTokensPerMinute` and `maxRequestsPerMinute` (whole numbers) pace what Tale sends this model within any one minute, for the organization in each Tale process. Set them to a hosted provider's per-minute limits, which are counted per provider account across every deployment that shares the key: an indexing batch that would cross a limit waits until enough of the last minute has aged out, so a crawl of hundreds of pages is paced instead of refused with `429`. Tale estimates tokens from characters, on the high side, keeps a sixtieth of each limit within any one second as well — a provider enforces its per-minute figure per second too — and sizes each request to fit that second. A `429` the provider still answers pauses every request of the organization to that model and is retried on a minute scale; a search query is not held back by either. Alibaba Cloud Model Studio, for example, allows text-embedding-v4 1,800 requests and 1,000,000 tokens per minute.
./docs/en/self-hosted/configuration/data-residency.md:74:Each embedding request has a ceiling: 15 minutes for indexing, and 5 minutes for a search query, which a chat answer waits on. Without `minTokensPerSecond`, Tale cannot tell how long the server's queue may take, so a request may use its whole ceiling. With it, Tale gives each request time for the work that can be ahead of it or beside it on the server, plus its own. That work is the request's own tokens, estimated generously from its characters, plus the other requests this Tale process may have in flight and `maxConcurrentRequests` more from other clients. Tale counts each of those requests as at least a full batch of 64 texts with 1,024 tokens each, divides the total by `minTokensPerSecond`, and adds 50%, but never allows less than 60 seconds or more than the ceiling. With the example above, a full batch of ordinary text gets about eight minutes, and a search query gets its five-minute ceiling. A search query also ends after five minutes in total, including any wait for a free slot. If more clients share the server than one more Tale process with the same limit, state a lower rate.
