ia point read, burned-broker-hash rotation over the consecutive-failed prefix, exhausted-walk = 'unknown' never "first start"); computed in the `task.agent_turn` job so the kick, the capacity wake, AND the auto-retry all inherit it from one seam. And AUTO-RETRY — `markTaskAgentRunFailed` arms `task.agent_retry` on its once-only terminal flip (same transaction; `isAutoRetryableFailure` reused — deadline/park_deadline/agent_deleted/agent_model_missing never arm) and the job re-derives every guard in one tx (task in_progress + still agent-assigned FOR UPDATE, failed run still newest by seq, `resolveAutoRetryBudget` reused: consecutive-short-failure streak ≤3 with the 15-min progress reset) then kicks with `trigger: 'auto_retry'` + the attempt stamp, attribution kept with the failed run's own starter. Integration: plan mechanics (first start/resume/failed-resume+rotation/incarnation fence) + a LIVE cascade (in_progress kick fails on the fake model → exactly 1+3 runs, attempts 1,2,3, then budget_exhausted) + negative lanes (deadline arms nothing; a guard miss kicks nothing). inc 29: the DRIVER/REVIEWER ARC — migration 0022 `app.approvals` (the generic 0.4 approvals ledger; first tenant `task_review`) + `domains/tasks/reviews.ts`: the settle's park to `in_review` mints ONE workflow-free review row in the flip's transaction (find-or-insert by runId — the burned-claim replay never double-mints; fresh mints supersede stale pending rows), reaching `in_review` through ANY human door (status route, drag) opens the same gate keyed on "one open gate per task", EVERY leave closes it (`closePendingTaskReviewOnStatusLeave` in all three status writers: a person's leave to done IS the approve — policy-checked, response recorded, `task.review_responded` audited; any system leave withdraws), and the respond door (`POST /tasks/reviews/:approvalId/respond`) approves (task → done AS THE RESPONDER, agents never reach done) or requests changes (feedback = task comment + verbatim `run.feedback` mention re-kick + the card handed back to In progress when no kick moved it). Reviewer resolution revalidates the 0.4 chain (designee → human task creator → project creator, first with canEdit); the org's `review_policy` file gates responders (independent-reviewer enforced against the run's `started_by`; a policy requiring competences refuses fail-closed until the competence-records domain lands); `GET /tasks/:taskId/review` feeds the sheet/chip. Integration: mint-once+replay, request-changes round-trip, round-2 mint + approve-completes, system-leave withdraw, and the policy refusal (403). inc 37 closes the bell gap: reviewer bells + subscription live (see collab). inc 81: the BOARD-CORE 0.4 wire-parity pass that let the frontend swap — list reads DECORATE rows (batch label resolution to catalog DTOs, folder-input facts via ONE recursive CTE over the folder subtree (active docs with a file; the 0.4 `collectFolderFacts`), the by-project/all-projects filters (status/statuses/assignee/externalSystem) + `truncated` cap + `canEdit`), NEW `GET /api/app/tasks` = the all-projects board (visible projects via the projects service, rows stamped `projectKey`), `getTask` answers the 0.4 envelope ({task, canEdit, canClaim, canComment}), dependencies answer FULL linked rows both ways + `GET /dependencies/by-project/:id` edges, the discussion read carries `{threadId, messages}` with mentions/bodyByLocale off the meta sidecar, activity rows the full 0.4 shape (org/task/project ids + createdAt — the timeline crashed on the missing clock, caught in the browser), `moveTask` takes NEIGHBOUR TASK IDS (ranks resolve server-side, rankBetween falls back to end-of-column), `claimTask` answers `{claimed, reason}` AS DATA (contested ≠ error), `deleteTask` deletes the SUBTREE (+ each task's discussion thread) returning `deletedChildCount`, `deleteTaskLabel` gates on in-use without `detach`, `POST /labels/ensure-defaults`; every task write hints (`task`, org-wide) via ONE hook in `recordActivity` (+ explicit on delete/labels); TASK_COLUMNS grew the shipped-but-unselected 0009 columns (cost/run counters, sla, thread ids). Frontend `app/lib/backend/tasks.ts`: 9 read + 19 write adapter rows, `taskView` null→absent projection. inc 82: the OPS TAIL — `getTaskOpsIndicators` per project (running task-agent runs + subject-linked live automation runs off `input->task->id` with the unexpired-pending-ask split → `askingTaskIds`; bounded newest-first scan) and the all-projects sibling (agent turns + pending reviews only — the 0.4 aggregate's own omission), routes under `/tasks/ops-indicators[/by-project/:id]` (FIXED paths registered BEFORE `/:taskId` — the param route swallowed them and the integration probe caught it), pending-review read projected to the 0.4 shape, agent-run list rows projected ({runId, agentSlug:=agentId, trigger∅→'manual', durationMs derived settled−launched, costCents 0 — per-run cost is not recorded on the pg row}), `POST /:taskId/agent-runs/cancel-live` (the 0.4 wire carries only the taskId — the live run resolves server-side), `setTaskReviewer` rides updateTask's reviewer field, collab subscription read/subscribe/unsubscribe/mute rows onto the existing `/collab/tasks/:id/subscription` pair. inc 83: the TASKS TAIL — `searchTasks` (token-AND over title/description/externalId/`KEY-number` via `LIKE ALL`, a comment-body fallback leg joining the discussion store, snippets capped 600; SQL searches the WHOLE visible set instead of 0.4's newest-80 Convex-read-limit window), `mentionTriggerPreview` (project agent-mode gate + the `task_automation` policy file; the breaker leg stays in the union but never fires — no pg pause bookkeeping), `bulkUpdateTasks` with the full 0.4 skip matrix (canEdit cache, archived content guard, open-subtask terminal skip, review-policy-refusal skip via the shared REFUSAL_CODES, live-run assignee-transfer gate over BOTH run families, per-project assignee-access probe reusing `assertAssigneeValid`), the manual `startTaskAgentRun` kick (assigned-agent resolve → `kickAgentRun`; reuse answers `already_running` as data), the run-card read (`GET /:taskId/agent-runs/latest` — agent name joined, `autoRetryMax` from the reused constant), the sandbox-op read (`GET /agent-runs/:runId/sandbox-op` — the run's own exec + `-`-suffixed incarnations on the standing session, fail-closed null), `GET /:taskId/live-automation-run` (the subject-linked live run off `input->task->>id`), `POST /:taskId/workflow/{start,cancel}` over the EXISTING `startWorkflowForTask` core + `cancelRun` + the cancelled-status park, `POST /from-external-issue` (the desk template-create: org-wide project fallback, `ensureFolder` minting the subject ROOT folder + setup-folder id onto externalUrl fail-closed, `upsertTaskByExternalRef` with user attribution, run-on-create inline), and `listAutomationsForApp` (the 0.4 APP listing wire — deployed-version taskContract/settings, scope filters — on `GET /automations/listing`; the REST v1 listing keeps its own shape). Frontend: 7 read + 5 write adapter rows (incl. the app-wide `automations/queries:listAutomations` row — the automations pages' listing starts working in hybrid for free). PENDING: steer lane (stdin/restart), live-turn re-attach recovery, run provenance ledger entries (`agent.run_settled` audit rows), approvals cleanup on task delete (0.4 parity: rows orphan). inc 108: the PROVENANCE LEDGER (`domains/tasks/run-ledger.ts`) — one immutable audit-chain entry per settled agent run, binding the run's artefacts to the model identity that served them, the capability scope it held at mint, the knowledge it read, and the reviewer it now waits on. It rides `app.audit_logs` (category `agent`), so chain verification, export and retention already cover it. Exactly-once BY CONSTRUCTION: each terminal transition (`settleAgentRun`/`failAgentRun`/`cancelAgentRun`) moved into a transaction whose `UPDATE … WHERE status IN ('queued','running') RETURNING` IS the settle election, and the ledger write shares it — a raced double-settle that degrades to a no-op writes no second entry (probed: the second settle returns false and the entry count stays 1). Attribution rules kept verbatim: deliverables filtered to THIS run, and the read-set attributed by the op's minted key so a sibling turn's reads on the same standing session are EXCLUDED — false provenance is worse than omission, and the full trail stays queryable on `sandbox_tool_calls`. Every enrichment is optional-tolerant (no op row, no token, refused review park → omitted field, never a failed settle). inc 110: the STEER LANE. The 0.4 steer body is HOISTED out of its `internalAction` into `steerTaskAgentTurnImpl` (0.4 suites green: 484/484) and runs on the ctx shim from a new `task.agent_steer` job — so the whole decision stays reused: stdin injection for a harness that takes input mid-turn, exec ROTATION for one that does not, its own tight-then-coarse retry ladder, and the settled-turn fallback that turns the comment into a fresh mention run. The shim's three deliberately-absent refs are now answered: `getOpSteerState` (is the exec still taking input), `rotateTaskAgentRunExec` (the SINGLE-WINNER claim — guarded on (running, fromExecId) so two concurrent steers cannot both rotate, and the superseded chain orphans itself because every settle mark is exec-guarded), and `kickMentionRunAfterSteerMiss`. The trigger is the comment door, now that mentions resolve (inc 106): a HUMAN comment naming the agent instance that owns a `running` run enqueues the steer — a queued run needs nothing (its start reads the brief after the comment posted) and an agent's own comment never steers itself. `task.agent_steer` carries `retryLimit: 0` on purpose: the host owns the retry ladder, and a second one on top would multiply steers. PENDING: live-turn re-attach recovery. inc 111 CLOSES the row: LIVE-TURN RE-ATTACH (`domains/tasks/reattach.ts`, run at the head of `watchdog.task_agents` — before the deadline pass, because a turn whose chain died is still doing work and failing it for a stale heartbeat would throw away a live agent's output). A turn is driven by a chain of jobs holding the drain; when that chain dies (backend restart, unhandled throw, lost reschedule) the sandbox keeps running the agent while the run sits at `running` with a silent op row, and nothing notices until the 12h deadline — which is only evaluated INSIDE the chain that died. The sweep lists runs whose op lease went silent (or that have no op row at all), PROBES the exec first (an unreachable spawner leaves everything for the next sweep — a transport hiccup is not evidence of a dead agent), CLAIMS the resume (closing the list→enqueue race; a refusal is logged, never silent — the original wedge hid behind a logless skip), and enqueues `task.agent_drive`, which runs the REUSED `driveTaskAgentTurnImpl`: it replays the exec's ring buffer and runs the same settle choreography a fresh start reaches, which is exactly why re-attaching is safe. All three 0.4 phase shapes heal: a missing op row is CREATED by the claim (the run row is the durable proof the turn exists), a terminal one is latched, a running one has a dead finalize winner's election re-opened. Probed with all three shapes plus the unreachable-spawner case. inc 112: row CLOSED — the steer lane (inc 110), provenance ledger (inc 108), re-attach recovery (inc 111) and the date ladder (inc 105) complete it. fix campaign 2026-09 (`fix/tasks-integrity`): the doors no client reached are RETIRED — `POST /tasks/bulk` + `bulkUpdateTasks`, the board-views trio (`app.board_views` stays: deprecated, no writer), `GET /tasks/pending-reviews` (the ops-indicator routes feed the chips) and the per-run cancel route (cancel-live is the app's only cancel) — and the respond door `POST /tasks/reviews/:approvalId/respond` + `respondToTaskReview` with them: the status doors ARE the decision (In review → Done approves under `review_policy`; back to In progress or an @-mention of the agent requests changes). The attachments the dialogs send are now WRITTEN (create/update accept the list; a ref new to the task must be the caller's own upload; dropped refs go through the same release seam as a hard delete). The host's settle/fail marks ride the ledgered `settleAgentRun`/`failAgentRunFromTurn` (one `agent.run_settled` per real run, outputs stamped with the producing run) and `launchAgentRun` is exec-fenced; migration 0080 makes one live run per task a partial unique index (the kick inserts ON CONFLICT and answers the winner as reused); the settle's park refuses a card a person moved (`TASK_MOVED`/`RUN_NOT_LIVE`) and the human status doors cancel the live run on leaving In progress; a card created straight at In progress kicks its run and one created at In review opens the gate. Date rungs claim+announce each row in one transaction, rescheduling re-arms the ladder, and the overdue nudge carries `bodyByLocale` (the comment lane now writes it). `startWorkflowForTask` guards one live run per (org, automation, task) under an advisory xact lock, the mention job carries a singletonKey, and only the undeployed case answers null (every other failure propagates); an archived task's external-ref reconcile is a no-op. Repeating tasks: migration 0130 adds `repeat_rule` (the rule, `lib/shared/task-repeat.ts`, with `createOn: 'dueDate'` when the next task also comes on the due date), `repeat_next_task_id` (FK ON DELETE SET NULL, unique partial index), `repeat_continued_at_ms` (stamped with the pointer and never cleared, so deleting a copy never re-arms the task before it: deleting a series' newest task ends the series, deleting a middle copy never forks it; the task rows read it as `repeatContinued`, which locks the board's Repeat row and makes the edit door refuse a rule), the `tasks_repeat_on_due` partial index and the `repeat` subscription reason. Migration 0132 adds durable series identity and ordered membership, recovers existing chains from their pointers and retained creation-audit ancestry, and keeps old-image continuations in the same series during rolling deployment; deleted ancestry with neither a surviving link nor retained audit cannot be reconstructed. One writer (`domains/tasks/repeat.ts`) decides under the task's row lock and creates exactly one next copy in To do — dated to the rule's next occurrence, rule carried, assignee/reviewer and watchers kept only while they still hold, subtasks (fresh, dated by the parent's step, internal dependencies redrawn) brought back — for a close from an open column through any status door (the settle seam; a savepoint that logs and never fails the close; the workflow engine's external-ref close continues no series) and for the `tasks.repeat_on_due` scan (every five minutes: an open task whose due day began in the rule's zone, credited to the system, at most ten open tasks per series); the stamp makes a reopen and re-close a no-op; an automation's task whose person can no longer carry over ends its series (rule cleared, `repeat.changed` credited to the lane's actor); subtasks and automation-owned tasks never carry a rule; `/status` and `/move` answer the copy as `nextTask`, and `POST /:taskId/repeat/stop` ends the series (taking back an untouched next task); REST v1 reads `startDate`/`dueDate`/`repeat`/`repeatNextTaskId` (contract 3.3.0) and the agent's `task_get` shows the dates and the rule |
./services/platform/backend/MIGRATION.md:168:| websites              | done                               | inc 67: migration 0045 `app.websites` (the org-facing registration/status/failure-ledger row; UNIQUE (org, domain)) + `domains/websites/{service,routes}.ts` + `rest/v1-websites.ts`. The corpus side stays in the KNOWLEDGE database untouched: the whole data seam (`knowledge/crawl.ts` — register/deregister/membership-guarded reads/BM25-with-ILIKE-fallback search) and the crawl ENGINE (`knowledge/crawl_action.ts`: discovery via robots/sitemaps/BFS, polite budgeted fetch with content-type dispatch, boilerplate paragraph-hash stripping, chunk + embed (NULL vectors when no embedding model — keyword-only until a re-scan), 404 prune, continuation chain with corpus-claim takeover) are REUSED VERBATIM — the two handler bodies hoisted (`scanWebsiteImpl`/`scanDueWebsitesImpl`, 0.4 wrappers delegate; suites 262+231 green) and run on a ctx shim: knowledge shim (org lookups + embedder), the sandbox session verbs behind the RENDER lane (`renderUrlsInSandbox` imported as-is; quota errors stay infra failures), this service's recordScanFailure/clearScanFailures/listWebsitesForScanScheduling handlers, and a scheduler seam mapping the self-chain onto `websites.scan` (retryLimit 0 — the engine records its own failures; the 5-min `websites.scan_due` schedule is the retry) and the row-sync fan-out onto singleton-keyed `websites.row_sync` jobs. Registration (`websites.register` job) = corpus upsert (site or curated URL LIST — list entries validated on-domain, re-post merges, kind only widens) + first-scan kick + best-effort homepage title/description + delayed row sync. The failure LEDGER ported exactly (TALE-PROJECT-106: failures recorded on the row because the corpus that failed is unreachable): attempts advance the scheduler clock (bounded retry window), 3 connection-class failures pause + notify org admins ONCE (`websiteScanPaused` bell), resume clears + re-kicks. Routes `/api/app/websites` (list keyset + filters + palette search, count, create, patch (interval syncs corpus cadence — the 0.4 CRAWLER_WEBSITE_NOT_FOUND branch is vestigial: the op silently no-ops for non-members), delete (best-effort deregister; last member takes the corpus domain), resume, sync-statuses (hourly debounce, transient states exempt), pages (with the 0.4 fetch-pages debounce via metadata.lastStatusSyncAt), chunks, search); REST `/api/v1/websites` family route-for-route with `rest_api.ts` (list/create incl. URL lists/get/patch/delete/pages/per-site sync answering 'syncing' fire-and-forget/search). Integration (fake site via global-fetch interception — safeFetch string-guards pass a public-looking domain): register→scan (3 pages, chunks, corpus completed, row synced active 3/3, pages/chunks/search reads), drift (hash flip re-indexes in place, 404 prunes page+chunks), the failure ledger end to end (pause bell exactly once, isDueForScan false while paused, resume clears), REST family + URL-list lane + delete deregistering the corpus. NOT in the harness: the sandboxed RENDER of html pages (needs the browser image — the lane is wired; text/document pages cover the in-process path)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       